      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a3.9.1',
          kind: "grammar",
          refs: ['g3.9.20', 'g3.9.23'],
          lemma: "λίθων … τέκνα",
          title: "Stones and sons — a pun under the Greek",
          body: "In the Aramaic John would have spoken, “stones” is **אַבְנַיָּא** (*ʾabnayyāʾ*) and “sons” is **בְּנַיָּא** (*bənayyāʾ*) — the threat is a rhyme: God can raise up *banim* from *abanim*. The Greek keeps the sense and loses the music, one of many places where the Gospels' Greek is visibly a translation of a Semitic voice (worth noticing, since it argues these sayings were not first composed in Greek). The rhyme carries the theology: descent guarantees nothing — a claim aimed here at Abraham's pedigree, and one this Gospel has already enacted, having opened with a genealogy whose grace kept arriving through outsiders. Standing in the right queue, John says, is not the same as turning around.",
        },
      ],
