      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a3.12.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g3.12.23', 'g3.12.26'],
          lemma: "ἄχυρον … πυρὶ ἀσβέστῳ",
          title: "The fire in the preaching",
          body: "There is no honest way to soften John: the axe, the winnowing-fork, the unquenchable fire are threats, and the Baptist means them. Two observations, not as anaesthetic but as accuracy. First, the image sorts a *harvest*, not an audience of strangers — wheat and chaff come from the same field, even the same stalk; the line John draws runs through Israel, and (as later chapters will insist) through persons, not merely between them. Second, notice what ch. 11 will record: John, imprisoned, hears what the Mightier One is actually doing — healing, preaching to the poor, no axe in sight — and sends to ask, *“Are you the one who is to come, or shall we look for another?”* The Gospel's own herald becomes its first doubter, precisely because Jesus failed to match the fire John promised. Matthew keeps both the sermon and the later bewilderment. A book doing propaganda would have kept only one.",
        },
      ],
