          title: "The voice — two scriptures fused, and a third withheld",
          body: "The words from heaven are a weave: **“my Son”** from Psalm 2:7 — the coronation formula of David's kings — and **“in whom I delight”** from Isaiah 42:1, the opening of the *Servant* songs, whose singer bears others' wounds and ends despised. Royal psalm plus suffering servant: the whole arc of this Gospel is compressed into one sentence before the ministry begins. And hovering behind **“beloved”** (ἀγαπητός) many hear Genesis 22:2 — “your son, your *beloved* son, Isaac” — the child walked up a mountain to be offered. Father, Son, Spirit descending: later doctrine will build its grammar from scenes like this one, but note the restraint — Matthew paints the tableau and defines nothing. The scene does not argue; it discloses. The arguing, this Gospel trusts, can come after the seeing.",
